# What the Docker build context must NOT carry.
#
# One file governs BOTH images, because both are built from the repository root - see ci.yml's
# `linux container build & test` job:
#
#   docker build -f Dockerfile.linux-test -t doctoolkit-linux-test .
#   docker build -f samples/Container/Dockerfile -t doctoolkit-sample .
#
# A .dockerignore is named after the Dockerfile only when it sits beside it as
# `<dockerfile>.dockerignore`; a root-level one applies to every build whose context is the root.
# Do not add a second copy next to samples/Container/Dockerfile - it would shadow this one for
# that image and the two would drift.
#
# THIS IS A CORRECTNESS RULE FIRST AND A SIZE RULE SECOND.
#
# Dockerfile.linux-test exists to prove ONE thing: that a clean image built from only the paths
# its COPY list names will restore, build and pass. Without this file, `COPY src/ tests/ samples/`
# swept every local `bin/` and `obj/` directory in alongside the sources - 56 of them, about
# 1.99 GB - so the container was handed the host's prebuilt assemblies and stale MSBuild assets.
# A source file, a props file or a whole project missing from the COPY list could still resolve
# against that output, and the job would report green on a tree that cannot actually build from
# what it lists. The check was only ever as honest as an empty working tree, and a maintainer's
# working tree is never empty. This is the same failure the Dockerfile's own comment records
# twice (PR #107's Directory.Build.props, then the SDK pin) - found by reading the file, which is
# not a process.
#
# The rule that makes the list below decidable: THE CONTEXT SHOULD LOOK LIKE A CLEAN CHECKOUT.
# Everything named here is either build output or local-only maintainer material - by definition
# absent from a fresh `git clone`, which is all a CI runner ever has. Nothing here is a source
# file, so excluding it can only remove what CI would not have had either.
#
# It is NOT generated from .gitignore and must not be. They answer different questions - one what
# may be published, the other what may be built from - and .gitignore withholds files (docs/,
# CLAUDE.md, BACKLOG.html) for a publication reason that says nothing about a build. Where they
# overlap, keep both in step by hand.

# ─── .NET build output ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The reason this file exists. `**/` matters: these sit one and two levels down, inside the
# directories that ARE copied.
**/bin
**/obj

# ─── Regenerated reports ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Test, mutation, benchmark and coverage output. Two TestResults/ directories sit INSIDE
# tests/*/ , which is copied - the others are at the root and merely bloat the context.
**/TestResults
StrykerOutput
BenchmarkDotNet.Artifacts

# ─── Packed output and the local feed ────────────────────────────────
# 1.37 GB on this machine, and a stray `.nupkg` in the context is the one artefact that could let
# a restore resolve THIS package from disk instead of from the project graph under test.
artifacts
local-feed
**/*.nupkg
**/*.snupkg

# ─── Generated docs site ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# docfx/_site is rendered output; docfx/api is YAML generated from the assemblies.
docfx/_site
docfx/api

# ─── Build-time-only Node packages ───────────────────────────────────
# Installed by the `commit-format` check (scripts/check-commit-parses.mjs) and reaching neither
# shipped package nor either image.
**/node_modules

# ─── Local-only maintainer material ──────────────────────────────────
# samples/CLAUDE.md is the one that is not merely cosmetic: it lives inside a copied directory,
# so without this line it ships into the image while being absent from every clean checkout.
**/CLAUDE.md
BACKLOG.html
DEVELOPMENT-CHECKLIST.md
docs
spike
.superpowers

# ─── VCS, worktrees, editor and OS cruft ─────────────────────────────
# Nothing in either image reads git history, and .worktrees/ can hold a second full checkout.
.git
.worktrees
.vs
.vscode
.idea
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
